<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
    The launcher icon's foreground: the W monogram, threaded.

    One continuous stroke for the letter and a second for the needle, because that
    is what the mark is — a length of thread that happens to spell a W, ending at
    the eye of the needle that drew it. Strokes rather than a filled silhouette for
    the same reason: a filled outline of a thread is a drawing of a ribbon.

    It replaces the t-shirt this file used to hold, which was drawn here because
    there was nothing to import — every icon in the app this project is a port of
    was a 1x1 transparent placeholder left over from the Expo template.

    The 108x108 viewport is the adaptive-icon canvas, of which very little is
    promised. The launcher masks it to a shape of its own choosing — a circle, a
    squircle, a teardrop — and each of those fits inside a circle of radius 36
    about the centre, so that circle, rather than the 72x72 square, is what the
    artwork has to stay within. The geometry below was fitted to it: the drawing is
    as large as it can be and still survive a round mask, which is why the numbers
    are not round ones. LauncherIconTest checks that it still is, and says how.

    Keep the same drawing in ic_launcher_monochrome.xml; the test holds the two
    files to each other. scripts/generate-launcher-icons.py reads this file and
    draws the Android 7 rasters from it, so those need nothing kept in step by
    hand — run it after changing anything here.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <!--
        The thread: the tail, the loop, the two valleys of the W, and the rise into
        the needle. Round caps and joins throughout — a thread has no corners. The
        tail ending in one of those caps rather than in a taper is the one liberty
        taken with the drawing, and it is deliberate: a taper is a filled shape,
        and everything here is a stroke.
    -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M23.3,65.9 C32.5,68.2 40.1,59.8 44.0,48.3 C47.0,39.1 37.8,30.7 31.7,36.8 C25.6,42.2 26.4,52.1 32.5,60.6 C36.3,67.5 38.6,75.1 44.0,79.0 C49.3,82.0 52.4,68.2 53.9,52.9 C55.5,42.9 60.1,42.2 60.8,52.9 C61.6,64.4 62.4,74.4 66.2,79.0 C70.0,82.8 75.4,77.4 76.9,66.7 C77.7,61.3 76.9,57.5 76.9,55.2"
        android:strokeColor="#96795E"
        android:strokeWidth="4.9"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:strokeLineJoin="round" />

    <!--
        The needle: the eye as a closed loop, then the shaft out of the top of it to
        the point. Thinner than the thread, which is what says one of them is steel.

        The eye is a stroked oval rather than a hole punched through a filled shape,
        so what shows through it is simply the background — there is no fill rule to
        get right and nothing to go wrong when the system tints the monochrome copy.

        At 48dp the eye closes up into a dot. That is the size at which the letter
        has to carry the icon alone, and it does; the needle is what the mark has at
        192dp and in the app, where there are pixels to spend on it.
    -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M80.5,45.1 C82.2,45.7 82.8,48.3 81.9,50.9 C81.0,53.5 78.9,55.1 77.2,54.5 C75.5,53.9 74.9,51.4 75.8,48.8 C76.7,46.2 78.8,44.5 80.5,45.1 Z M80.5,45.1 L83.4,36.8"
        android:strokeColor="#96795E"
        android:strokeWidth="2.6"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:strokeLineJoin="round" />
</vector>
